<speak>
<p>Let the n coefficients of h be positive or negative one, and form the circulant matrix from that row and its successive right cyclic shifts. Define h star by replacing X with X inverse. We claim that H times H transpose equals n times the identity if and only if h times h star equals n, in the integer group ring of the cyclic group.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>First, suppose the matrix is Hadamard. The first row is orthogonal to every nontrivial cyclic shift. By the correlation identity, every nonconstant coefficient of h times h star is therefore zero. The constant coefficient is the sum of the squares of the row entries. Every coefficient is a sign, so this sum is n. The product is exactly the constant polynomial n.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Conversely, start with n sign coefficients and assume their polynomial satisfies h times h star equals n. Form the circulant matrix from those coefficients. Uniqueness of the representative of degree less than n says that each nonconstant coefficient of the product is zero. Thus the first row is orthogonal to every nontrivial cyclic shift.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Why does that give every pair of rows? Number rows from zero. Compare row r and row s, then shift both back by r places. A common shift does not change a dot product. We are left with row zero and the shift by s minus r. In formulas, substitute k equal to j minus r. The resulting sum is the coefficient of X to the s minus r in h times h star. It is zero for distinct rows, and n for equal rows. Hence H times H transpose is n times the identity.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>The sign assumption is part of the dictionary. Forward, it makes the squared norm exactly n. Backward, it guarantees that every entry of the constructed matrix is positive or negative one. The polynomial equation alone does not guarantee those entries.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>For example, in dimension four take the constant polynomial two. Its product with its star is four. But its coefficient list is two, zero, zero, zero. The circulant matrix is twice the identity: orthogonal rows, yet its entries are not signs. It is not Hadamard.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>For another useful check, take the all ones row in dimension four. Every shift is the same row, so two distinct rows have dot product four. The polynomial product has every coefficient equal to four, rather than only its constant coefficient. Circulant symmetry alone does not give orthogonality.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>With the sign hypothesis included, both implications are proved. Many matrix conditions have become one polynomial equation. In the next main node, we will ask what that equation tells us about the order n.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
